From the lib/ review after k5 (repro scripts in the session scratchpad: shared.pl, chain.pl). (1) Ownership label holds only the Comb name, not its namespace: two same-named Combs in different namespaces (the normal getty/dev layering in one cluster) prune each other objects when those live outside the own namespace or are cluster-scoped. Fix: add an owner-namespace label next to the comb label on every managed resource; prune only when both match. (2) Pruning cluster-scoped kinds cascades (a dropped Namespace takes every Comb in it, a dropped CRD takes every CR of that type cluster-wide). Fix: never prune cluster-scoped resources automatically; drop them from the record and say so in the Ready condition message ("left in place"). (3) Loop detection by own context name gives false positives: one context, namespaces getty -> dev -> prod gives via [c, c] -> Blocked UpstreamLoop; context names also collide across kubeconfigs (default, kind-kind). Replace the context-name check by a via length cap (configurable attribute, sensible default e.g. 16) -> Blocked UpstreamLoop "chain too long, loop?"; keep via bounded so it never grows without limit. Remove the k5 via-cutting logic if the cap makes it redundant. (4) An upstream class from the CR is constructed before the Role::Upstream check (CR can make the manager call Any::Class->new(%keys)). Fix: load, check DOES Kubernetes::Comb::Role::Upstream, then new - like _load_comb_class does for spec.class. (5) Stub contract check is skipped when spec.class names the stub directly (SPEC §9 requires it at construction). Fix by the stub_class naming convention: a class named Foo::Stub that isa Foo (a Comb class) is checked against Foo. (6) Kubernetes rejects values we emit unchecked: Comb name longer than 63 chars as a label value; endpoint names used as Service/EndpointSlice port names must be DNS-1123 labels (lowercase alnum and -, max 63). Fail early with a clear message (construction / Endpoint validation) instead of a 422 on every call. Regression test for each.

